~
    BBS commands:

  [33mCTRL-S[0m  [32mSuspend text listings[0m
  [33mCTRL-Q[0m  [32mStart text listings[0m
  [33mCTRL-C[0m  [32mAbort text listings[0m

When logging on The BBS,  ~N ,
if you do not wish to see the log on screen type the following:

Welcome to AmiExpress BBS version Beta
Your connected at XXXXX baud
ANSI Graphics ([32mY[0m/[37mN[0m)? {Enter [32mY[0m or [37mN[0m followed by [33mQ[0m for quit opening}

      [36m'A' [35m- [33mAlter file flags[0m
             This command allows you to change the flagged file list
             without having to do a file listings and get a PAUSE...MORE
             prompt to change the flags. This is good only for the current
             conference that you're in.
             Chaining data is allowed, or the routine will prompt you.

              First it will show the current list of flags.

              No file flags
              [36mFilename(s) to flag: [32m([33mC[32m)[36mlear[0m, [32m([33mEnter[33m)[0m=[36mnone[0m?

              At this prompt you may:
               1. Enter new files to add to the flag list
               2. Enter [36m'C'[0m to goto the clear flag prompt

              [36mFilename(s) to Clear: [32m([33m*[32m)[36mAll[0m, [32m([33mEnter[32m)[0m=[36mnone[0m?

              At this prompt you may:
               1. Enter filenames to remove from the flag list
                  wildcards are valid, however they will only
                  remove the first entry.
               2. Using '[36m*[0m' will remove all enties in the list.
               3. Or ([36mEnter[0m) to exit.

              Once you've exitied the Clear prompt it will once again show
              the list of flagged files and drop back to the Main Menu.

       [36m'B' [35m- [33mBulletins[0m
             Displays a list of Bulletins if available in the current
             conference.  Choose a bulletin number to read or ([36mEnter[0m) to
             return to Main Menu.

             [37mStacked command[0m:  [33mB 1  [32mRead Bulletin 1[0m

      [36m'C' [35m- [33mComment to SYSOP[0m
             This command is the same as entering a message, except the
             message is to the SYSOP.

      [36m'D' [35m- [33mDownload a file[0m
             You can only download files that are in the conference you're
             currently in.  You can't flag files in one conference, then
             join a different conference and still download those flagged
             files.

             1. The BBS displays the your download/upload stats.

             2. The BBS then figures out what ratio limits you have left
                before uploading.

             3. Next it asks for the protocol to be used.  At this time
                Z (Z-Modem) is the only valid protocol.

             4. Wildcards are allowed, but not without at least one normal
                character.  EG: "*" is not allowed but "a*" is allowed.

             5. As the BBS finds a file and displays the amount of time
                required and how big, if you can d/l it, the BBS
                automatically checks the flaglist and if there isn't
                already a entry like the one just found, then it adds it
                to the flag list.  This is so if you want to exit the d/l
                routine to list a file area again, you don't have to type
                the filenames in again.  If you need to remove a file you
                don't want to download, just use [36m'A'[0mlter flags command.

             6. After hitting ([36mEnter[0m) on a blank filespec prompt the BBS
                displays the totals and checks to see if you can download
                the files within your limits.

             7. Asks "([36mEnter[0m) to Start, ([36mG[0m)oodbye after transfer, ([36mA[0m)bort?"
                Specifing [36m'A'[0mbort will exit out of the download routine.
                [36m'G'[0moodbye after transfer will give you 10 secs to decide
                whether you really want to logoff or not.

             [37mStacked command[0m:  [33mD [32mFilename1 Filename2 Filename3[0m

      [36m'E' [35m- [33mEnter a message.[0m
             If you're addressing a person who doesn't have access to the
             conference you're in, they will never get that message.

             1. Asks for who to send it to.  If a wildcard is used the
                search will find the first user that fits the wildcard and
                will display it and then ask if its correct, if you say no,
                then it will search forward.  If it doesn't find the user,
                it exit back to the Main Menu prompt.

             2. If you just hit ([36mEnter[0m) on the TO: prompt it will send
                the message to ALL.  This will be a public message.

             3. Another name that is valid in the TO: prompt is SYSOP, this
                will be replaced by the Name of the SYSOP, in user slot 1.

             4. Next prompt is the Subject of the message, if you just
                hit ([36mEnter[0m) it will exit back to the Main Menu prompt.

             5. The BBS will now ask if the message is to be readable only
                by the receiver of the message.

             6. Now you have entered the message editor.

             7. Tabs now work in the editor, they are indicated by a '|' in
                the header.

             8. Ctrl-X in the message editor will delete the current line.

             9. If you hit ([36mEnter[0m) on a blank line you will exit the
                message editor to the Edit Command prompt.

            10. The options are:

                 1. [32mA[33m)[36mbort[0m  - Abort entering the message.

                 2. [32mC[33m)[36mont[0m   - Continue entering the message, this option
                              will print the last line with text and put
                              the cursor at the end of the line.

                 3. [32mD[33m)[36melete[0m - Allows you to delete a specific line.

                 4. [32mE[33m)[36mdit[0m   - Allows you to change text within a line.

                 5. [32mL[33m)[36mist[0m   - List the message to the screen.

                 6. [32mS[33m)[36mave[0m   - Saves the message.


      [36m'F' [35m- [33mFile listings[0m
             File listing will only show files that are available in the
             conference you are currently in.

             1. A listing of file directories will be displayed.  Type the
                directory number to see the list of files, or ([36mEnter[0m) by
                itself to return to the Main Menu prompt.

             2. The specified directory will be displayed and will pause
                unless [36mNS[0m (Non-Stop) has been specified.

             3. On the pause prompt, you can select [36m'F'[0m for flagging files.

             [37mStacked commands[0m:   [33mF 1[0m  [32mList Files in directory 1[0m
                                 [33mF * NS[0m  [32mList Files in non-stop mode![0m
                                       (Where * = A(ll) U(pload) or dir #)

      [36m'G' [35m- [33mGoodbye[0m

             1. First checks to see if there was a ZooMail that hasn't been
                downloaded.  If there is, it will ask if you want to leave
                anyway.  NOTE: ZooMail has been disabled temporarily.

             2. The system then checks to see if there are any partial
                uploads that the you haven't finished sending yet.  If so,
                then it will let you know and ask if you want to leave
                anyway.  If you don't want to leave, then the BBS asks if
                you want to view the files.  If you do want to view them,
                the BBS will go into the resume routine showing you the
                filename and filesize, then ask if you want to resume on
                that particular file.  If not, the the BBS asks if you
                want to delete the file.  If you don't want to delete that
                file, then the BBS will go onto the next file that is to
                be resumed.

             3. The BBS will display the Log-Off screen.

      [36m'H' [35m- [33mHelp[0m

             1. The BBS will display this file.

      [36m'J' [35m- [33mJoin[0m

             1. The BBS will display a list of conferences that you have
                access to.  Type the number of the conference you want to
                go to or ([36mEnter[0m) to return to the Main Menu prompt.

             [37mStacked command[0m:  [33mJ 1  [32mJoin Conference 1[0m

      [36m'M' [35m- [33mMode Select[0m
            This option allows you to toggle between ANSI color and
            monochrome display.

      [36m'N' [35m- [33mNew files since.[0m
            This allows you to list just the files that have been added to
            the BBS since the last time you've called.  This will only show
            new files for the conference that you're currently in.

             1. It will then ask for the date you want to list from.
                The last date you were on will be the default.  This is
                selected by just hitting ([36mEnter[0m) alone.  Entering the date
                to start the list from must be done as MM-DD-YY.

             2. Next the BBS asks for the directory to search thru.  An [36m'A'[0m
                can be used to list all directories, also [36m'U'[0m can be
                specified for the Upload directory.

             3. The Pause prompt works the same as described in the file
                listing section.

                          ([33mN[0m)ew Files [36mDate[0m & [37mDir #[0m
             [37mStacked commands[0m:  [33mN [36m06-01-89 [37m6[0m
                                [33mN [36m06-01-89 [37m6 [32mNS[0m (Non-Stop)

      [36m'O' [35m- [33mOperator Page[0m
            This function is used to try to page the SYSOP to see if he's
            around to chat.

             1. If you select this option you name displayed in the status
                bar will change to a different color even if the paging
                function is currently turned off.

             2. If the Chat toggle flag is not set to allow paging then the
                BBS will say:

                 "Sorry, The SYSOP, is not around right now
                  You can use [36m'C'[0m to leave a comment."

             3. If the chat toggle flag is set to allow paging, then the
                BBS displays "paging SYSOP (CTRL-C to Abort). ." followed
                by bells and more dots.

             4. If the SYSOP is around and wants to chat with you, he will
                see the flashing and initiate the chat function.  During
                chat, if ANSI mode is selected, your text will be a
                different color than his.

      [36m'OPEN' [35m- [33mOpen a door[0m
             Displays a list of doors available in the current conference.
             Type the number of the door you want to run or ([36mEnter[0m) to
             return to the Main Menu prompt.

             [37mStacked command[0m:  [33mOPEN 1  [32mStart door 1[0m

      [36m'R' [35m- [33mRead mail[0m
             First the BBS shows the read prompt:

                [33mA[32m>[36mgain [33mD[32m>[36melete [33mR[32m>[36meply [33mQ[32m>[36muit [32m<[33mCR[32m>[0m=[36mNext[0m [32m([0ma+b[32m)[0m?

             Hitting ([36mEnter[0m) by itself will show the next available public
             message.  You can also jump to a message by typing a message
             number:
                     ([37m#[0m)  Will take you straight to that message.
                     ([37m+[0m)  Read messages Forward order.
                     ([37m-[0m)  Read messages Reverse order.

                 1. [33mA[32m>[36mgain[0m displays the message currently shown again,
                    (specified by the 'a' in the above line).

                 2. [33mD[32m>[36melete[0m allows you to delete a message only if you
                    left the message or the message is to you.

                 3. [33mR[32m>[36meply[0m allows you to reply to either a private message
                    you've just read, or a public (ALL) one.

                 4. [33mQ[32>[36muit[0m exits out of the read mail routine.

                 [37mStacked commands[0m:

                         [33mR 10+  [32mRead at message 10 and go forwards.[0m
                         [33mR 10-  [32mRead at message 10 and go backwards.[0m
                         [33mR NS   [32mRead new messages in non-stop mode.[0m

      [36m'S' [35m- [33mStats for the User[0m
            This function shows you some of your stats.

                            (123456789)
                [32mConf Access[33m:[0m XXXXXXXXX
                [32mCaller Num.[33m:[0m 2049
                [32mLst Date On[33m:[0m 05-14-91
                [32mSecurity Lv[33m:[0m 30
                [32m# Times On [33m:[0m 579
                [32mRatio DL/UL[33m:[0m Disabled
                [32m# Downloads[33m:[0m 0
                [32m# Uploads  [33m:[0m 0
                [32mBytes DL'd [33m:[0m 0
                [32mBytes UL'd [33m:[0m 0
                [32mBytes Avail[33m:[0m 99999999

      [36m'T' [35m- [33mTime[0m
            This function shows the current time and date at the BBS.

      [36m'U' [35m- [33mUpload[0m
            This function allows you to send files to the BBS.

             1. The BBS shows the amount of free space.  If lower then
                2,000,000 bytes in the current directory (The second
                number.), the BBS will not allow any uploads.

             2. At this point the BBS checks to see if you have any
                incomplete uploads.  This works the same as explained under
                the [36m'G'[0moodbye command.

             3. The BBS then asks you to enter the filenames of the files
                you wish to upload.  Pressing ([36mEnter[0m) alone will have
                Z-Modem tell the BBS the names.  Specifing an [36m'A'[0m will
                abort out of the upload and return you to the Main Menu
                prompt.

             4. If you enter filenames, the BBS will check to make sure
                they don't already exist.  If not, the BBS will prompt you
                to enter a description for each file.  Enter a blank line
                to end the description.

             5. Next the BBS will display:

                ([36mEnter[0m) to Start, ([36mG[0m)oodbye after transfer, ([36mA[0m)bort?

               This works the same as specified in the download routines.


             6. Once the transfer has completed the BBS displays the stats
                from the transfer for all the files and the time used is
                credited for the uploads.

             7. The BBS checks files sent to see if the name is longer than
                12 characters and asks you to rename them if any are.  You
                will also be prompted for file descriptions if you've let
                Z-Modem tell the BBS the file names.

             8. Once the description has been entered the BBS checks to see
                if the file ends in .ARC .ZOO .LZH .WRP or .DMS and if
                there is, the BBS tests the file.

      [36m'V' [35m- [33mView a file[0m
            This function will allow you to view (read) a text file that is
            in the file listings.

             1. The BBS asks for the filename to view.  The BBS will only
                display a plain, uncompressed text file.  Files with
                characters greater than 127 cause the BBS to return the
                message: "This file is not a text file." and then returns
                back to the Main Menu prompt

             [37mStacked command[0m:  [33mV [32mFilename.txt[0m

      [36m'W' [35m- [33mWrite info[0m
            This function allows you to change a couple of items about
            yourself.

                1. Name     : Joseph Hodge
                2. From     : Radford, VA
                3. Phone    : 703-639-6114
                4. Password : BuggyBoy
                5. NumLines : 23
                6. Computer : Amiga 2000/GVP030
                7. Scr Type : Amiga ANSI
                8. Scrn Clr : Y
                9. Protocol : /X Internal Zmodem
                10.Editor   : Prompt

      [36m'X' [35m- [33mExpert mode toggle[0m

            This function toggles whether you are in expert mode or novice
            mode.  In novice mode the BBS will redisplay the menu after
            every command you issue.

      [36m'Z' [35m- [33mZippy search[0m

             1. The BBS will ask for the string to search for, this search
                is NOT case sensitive.  If you just press ([36mEnter[0m), it will
                exit back to the Main Menu prompt.

             2. Next it will ask for the directory to search thru, options
                for this prompt are given above under ([36mN[0m)ew files since.

      [36m'ZOOM' [35m- [33mZoo Mail[0m

            This function will put all unread messages, both private to you and
            public, into one file.  The BBS will then download the file to you
            using your default protocol setting.


␀[37mNOTEABLE FEATURES:[0m

     1. Command history has been added in to allow up to 20 history lines
        to be remembered.  Once the limit of 20 has been released the
        history will cycle thru.  [33mCTRL-B[0m will reset the history pointer
        to zero entries.


     2. Automatic Z-Modem upload.  If you start a Z-Modem upload on the
        Main prompt, the BBS will detect this and enter the Z-Modem
        upload routines.


 [36m*** HST users ***[0m

 For maximum speed make sure you run Amimodem or another similar program
 to set the HST up for maximum file transfers.  If you have a HST 9600 you
 can expect around 1200CPS average time.  If you have a 14.4K HST you can
 expect around 1450-1650CPS average time depending on the phone
 connection.  How do you tell if you have a 14.4K or 9600 HST?  Well after
 loging off from the BBS, give your modem the following AT command:

 ATI6
 USRobotics Courier 9600 HST Dual Standard Link Diagnostics...

 Chars sent                 1168      Chars Received             6840
 Chars lost                    0
 Octets sent                1168      Octets Received            6119
 Blocks sent                1027      Blocks Received            1642
 Blocks resent                 1

 Retrains Requested            2      Retrains Granted              0
 Line Reversals                0      Blers                       196
 Link Timeouts                 0      Link Naks                     0

 Data Compression       On
 Equalization           Long
 Fallback               Enabled
 Speed                  14400/450

 Disconnect Reason is Loss of Carrier

 OK

 If the speed says 14400/450 you have a 14.4K HST if it says 9600/450 you
 have one of the older 9600 HST's.  Call US Robotics about upgrading to a
 14.4K HST.

